{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"just_cause_eviction","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act: eviction only for listed causes, with notice to quit","requirement":"A landlord may bring an eviction suit only on one of the causes the Anti-Eviction Act lists, and must first serve a Notice to Quit. The notice period depends on the cause: 3 days for employment-based tenancy, drug, assault or theft convictions; 1 month for lease-term changes or habitual late payment; 2 months for owner-occupancy after conversion; 18 months to retire the property from residential use; and 3 years for condominium, cooperative or fee-simple conversion. Landlords must give a Notice to Cease before suing for habitual late payment. A tenant evicted for illegal occupancy is owed relocation assistance of six times monthly rent. A landlord who removes a tenant to occupy the unit must occupy it for at least 6 months. A landlord who removes a tenant to retire the property must not use it residentially for 5 years. Penalties include treble damages plus fees and a civil penalty of up to $10,000 per violation. Rooming and boarding houses and public housing authorities must also follow these eviction rules.","citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"If the tenant continuously fails to pay rent or habitually pays late, after written Notice to Cease, the landlord may file a suit for eviction.","doc_kind":"agency_guidance","enacted_date":null,"effective_date_text":null,"key_value":"Notice to Quit from 3 days to 3 years depending on cause; eviction only for statutory causes","exemptions":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings are governed by N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2, which lists a narrower set of causes: lease expiration, non-payment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, repeated rule violations, and violation of a lease provision that reserves a right of re-entry.","Owners of three or fewer residential units, or three or fewer condominium or cooperative units